{"path": "SECURITY_REVIEW.json", "content": "{
  "findings": [
    {
      "category": "ssrf",
      "severity": "CRITICAL",
      "files": [
        "src/origin.js",
        "src/fetcher.js"
      ],
      "evidence": "The URL allowlist is intended to restrict requests to the trusted origin 'https://trusted.example', but allowedPrefix() in src/origin.js only performs a textual prefix check: `url.startsWith('https://trusted.example')`. This is bypassable. Tracing the user-controlled value: src/fetcher.js fetchPreview() reads `const url = String(req.query.url || '')` from the request, gates it with `if (!allowedPrefix(url)) throw`, and then passes the same unmodified `url` to `fetch(url)`. Because the check is a string prefix rather than a parsed-origin comparison, the following attacker-supplied values pass the allowlist yet contact a different host: (1) 'https://trusted.example.evil.com/' passes the prefix but fetch() resolves host to 'trusted.example.evil.com' (attacker-controlled subdomain); (2) 'https://trusted.example@evil.com/' passes the prefix (the 'trusted.example' portion is treated as userinfo), but URL parsing shows fetch() contacts hostname 'evil.com' with userinfo 'trusted.example' ignored — giving the attacker full control of the destination; (3) 'https://trusted.example.evil.com:443/' similarly bypasses. Using the userinfo form the attacker can target any host after the '@', including internal addresses such as 'https://trusted.example@169.254.169.254/' (cloud metadata) or 'https://trusted.example@127.0.0.1:PORT/', making the server perform arbitrary outbound/inbound requests (full SSRF). Verified at runtime: allowedPrefix() returns true for all three bypass payloads, and `new URL(payload).hostname` confirms the contacted host is attacker-controlled ('evil.com' / 'trusted.example.evil.com'), not 'trusted.example'.",
      "remediation": "Validate the parsed URL origin rather than a textual prefix. Parse the input with the URL API, confirm the scheme is exactly 'https:', and compare the parsed hostname to the trusted host using a strict full-host equality (or a well-tested suffix match that also ensures a leading dot for subdomains). For example: `const u = new URL(url); if (u.protocol !== 'https:' || u.hostname !== 'trusted.example') throw new Error('untrusted origin'); return fetch(u.toString());`. Additionally, reject URLs containing userinfo (username/password) and consider resolving the hostname to block private/loopback/link-local ranges before fetching to harden against SSRF to internal targets."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": [
    "src/origin.js",
    "src/fetcher.js"
  ],
  "source_modified": false
}
"}

